A||N||21||Monkeyman||Push button starter||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||03-04-2001||05:42 AM||||Here's what I want to do:  Use a toggle switch for ignition/power (looking at one of those neat red covered, aircraft style ones) and a push button starter switch.<P>Security shouldn't be a problem since I plan on installing an alarm system.  <P>How should I wire things up?  Do I need to use relays?  How can I lock out the starter switch once the motor is running?  I want to eliminate the key altogether.<P>I want to be able to reverse things if I ever decide to reuse the key switch if possible.<P>Any help would be appreciated.<P>MM||||reg||5
Z||000001||Wolfhound||03-04-2001||06:37 AM||||MM, You may be able to find a three way rocker switch that toggles to on and when pressed furthur acts as a momentary switch for your starter.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rprevo||03-04-2001||06:50 AM|||| If the togle switch is going to be used<BR>stricly for the power to the coil you shouldn't need a relay but make sure it is fused and the wire you use is the same<BR>thickness as the original wire. With the stater button your going to need pretty thick wire not sure of the size but the back of the box that the push button comes in will tell you the amp rateing of the wire to use.<BR> As far as keeping it so you may return original just run new wire and leave the old disconnected and tucked out of the way.<BR>On the garage floor take a Battery a starter and a togle switch and wire it up try every thing<BR>in that fasion before you hardwire it to the<BR>car and don't forget to fuse the stuff!<BR>The only thing the pushbutton will do is take the place of the key being turned all the way to start position. So your original little wire on your starter will need to be disconected and insulated. Same with your<BR>wire that powers your coil you only diconnect it and conceal it and use your new wireing.<BR>All you need is a terminal block from radio shack that you can pick your 12 volts up   from your battery and you will be in bussiness.<BR> Remember, Test the circuit on the floor before you put it in the car. <BR> Thats my 2 cents<BR> <BR>No Fieros were harmed in the makeing of this<BR>post!<P><BR>   Ray<BR>  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||03-04-2001||06:57 AM||||Thanx for the suggestion, but I really want the setup I described.  I just have to figure out how to wire everything up.<P>The illuminated starter switch is $70 through JCWhitney and the covered switch is less than $20 through Summit.  <P>I plan on modifying the shifter console a bit to mount these.  Somehow I want to replace the top of the shifter console with a piece of black Lexan.  I'll mount the switches on one side of the shifter.  I'd like to have an ashtray on the other side but I'm unsure of how to mount the stock one.  I might have to find another kind in a boneyard somewhere.  Solidly mounting it will be the only problem.  Don't know how to do that and keep away from visible screw/bolt heads.||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||03-04-2001||10:13 AM||||If you want security, how about putting the switch on the floor like they used to do on old cars (my '51 Chevy truck has the starter switch on the floor)...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||03-04-2001||11:16 AM||||MM,That's a lot of money for a switch.If you find a cheaper switch that you like you can use it to control a small Bosch relay that will work just fine.It will also be just as easy to hook up.I'll be glad to help with the wires.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||03-04-2001||11:22 AM||||Some other type of switch might work as well, but I liked the looks of the illuminated switch.  (See <A HREF="http://www.tdsasports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tdsasports.com</A>  to see the switches I'm looking at.)  Once I get the hardware, I'll definitely need help with the wiring.  I just want to be sure it's not going to be a professional mechanics job.  If I can't do it myself with my limited mechanical ability, I won't do it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||03-04-2001||01:44 PM||||mm, you have a bunch of circuits to deal with. There are a whole bunch of things tied to the OE switch and all of them will need to be accounted for. The injector(s), ECM, ignition, dash gauges, radio, cruise controls, and other things are run thru there.||||reg||1
Z||000008||tgowens||03-04-2001||02:47 PM||||MM, theogre is right but in addition to that, right now the ignition is connected to the key/lock assembly by a rod going thru the steering column to a switch located at the base of the column. You can bypass that easily.........also, remember, we use remote starter switches all the time here at the shop........those connect directly to the starter........<P>Just some things to think about.........||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||03-04-2001||03:08 PM||||Can't I just tap into some wiring for this?  If it's going to be too complicated (read:  over my head), I won't do it.  I was under the assumption that I would only have to reroute a couple of wires for each switch.  Hmmm...<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||03-04-2001||04:21 PM||||No. it's more complicated than that. you have to acount for nearly all the wires on the OE switch. You'll have to got thru each circuit on the OE switch, figure out what it does, then build new controls for it.<P>The actual starter switch can be any decent switch. You are only activating the relay on the starter.<P>Keep in mind, when this is done to race cars, most of those cars don't have computer FI engines. The ones the do have several sitches and/or relays to control the computer, injector power, fuel pump, etc.<P>It could be done but won't be simple. It will be dificult to do safely and reliably. There's more to it security wise than an alarm preventing someone taking off with it. If you leave a swicth on after working on something you could drain the battery or even burn up some parts. Could potentially be a fire hazard.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||03-04-2001||06:01 PM||||Oh, well.  Better to know I can't do it now then once I had purchased the parts.  On to other projects.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||03-04-2001||06:13 PM||||Well, the easiest way to achieve your desired look (not necessarily the full functionality) would be to just add the toggle switch and push button starter switch in addition to the OEM key.  Put the toggle and push button in series together and run them to the starter relay.  You'd still have to switch your OEM key to 'on' but you could then enable and start the car with the switches.  Kill the remote switch and the starter button won't work.  You would still be able to start the car with just the key, though.  This is more of a show setup than full functionality.||||reg||6
Z||000013||Greg Piet||03-04-2001||06:46 PM||||Im with formula88 on this one...<P>I have had my ignition switch stick so it will not turn over, but everything else worked.  This would be a good secondary for that as well.  <BR>It also keeps the steering wheel locked which is good I suppose... <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||MoneypitGT||03-04-2001||08:20 PM||||The only wires you need to energize are the orange and pink wires coming from your main ignition harness, they are under you steering column to the right side wrapped in tape. All the wire are about 12ga.The starter wire is yellow that goes to purple, depending where you get it. I did a simliar setup on my friends '00 Si, we hid a switch and push button above the power outlet, worked awesome. I would suggest using relays.<BR>   Chris<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fieroproject/moneypitgt.gif">   <B>Check out my site at:</B> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fieroproject" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fieroproject</A> <BR>2 '87 GT Projects||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||03-04-2001||11:05 PM||||I'd be careful about having no other security. It takes me about 20 seconds to kill a standard Clifford 3-channel alarm. A real professional would be driving away by that time. I'd wire through the existing starter circuit, and splice off the ignition wires coming off the drum. Run the extension to the new panel, and voila: Switches that work the way you want to, but need a key to work.||||reg||4
Z||000016||007DOUG||03-05-2001||12:39 AM||||I've done this "conversion" before using the wire tap method on an 84 4cyl automatic.  <P>Its VERY easy, just drop the column, tap into the CORRECT igntion wires (use the diagrams in the rear of your haynes/chiltons), and install the switches.<P>My fiero didn't have a rear trunk release. This was perfect for mounting the switches. A good "security" method would be mounting the push button in the ashtray. I've done this method for the valet switch on my alarm.   <P>The flip switch was wired into all igntion accessories (just like turning your key on, but not starting the car).<P>The push button was wired to the starter relay. (just like turning your key all the way to start the car).<P>When you wanted to start the car: flip the switch, push the button, and voila.<P>To shut the car off, flip the switch. <P>ONLY ONE PROBLEM: Don't use Radio Shack push buttons. I burnt-up one too many. Even when wired to just a relay, the starter pulls alot of juice. Its gotta be a heavy duty push button switch. RS flip switches work fine.<P><P>------------------<BR>5 Indy pace Cars<BR>84 T-top<BR>86 SE V6 153K (daily)<BR>86 GT red <BR>86 GT Yellow ASAP<BR>86 GT w/4:10 tranny<BR>86 GT 3.4 Pearl White T-top<BR>88 Coupe<BR>88 Formula 5 speed (soon)<BR>2 88 GT's (twins)<P>But......<BR><I>"Time is my most precious and most perishable of all my possessions."</I>||||reg||14
Z||000017||StuGood||03-05-2001||08:07 PM||||Well, I wouldn't chicken out yet.  Just be sure and plan carefully before you make a move.  Wiring diagrams for the Fiero are available on the web - you can make use of them.<P>And, if/when you decide to dive in and try it, make everything you do be reversible - so you can go back to the way it was before you messed with it.  And try to anticipate everything that could go wrong, and what the consequences would /might be.<P>That's (part of) what I went through when I developed the "Winky" headlight.  It works great, but it sure didn't happen overnight   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <P>BTW, regarding cheap Radio Scrap pushbutton switches (the little red ones), on another little project years ago, I found it <B>was</B> possible to reliably crank a starter using one of those!  The trick was, I used a horn relay in series to turn the starter <B>off</B>.  As an old Physics prof taught us, "The trick isn't getting hold of the tiger - it's letting go."         <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 03-05-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000018||pHoOl||03-06-2001||09:39 PM||||I have been thinking about doing a "blinky" headlight switch myself.  Please email me with any info and wiring diagrams that you have from when you did it.  I'm assuming from the title, you're doing it so that when you have the headlights on, one of the headlights will blink down and back up as if you were winking at that beautiful woman that just crossed the crosswalk or something.<P><BR>janikm@river.it.gvsu.edu<P>thanks much<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Hott86||03-07-2001||06:19 PM|||| I know JC Whitney has some. <A HREF="http://www.jcwsportcompact.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jcwsportcompact.com</A>  & u should find some.<P>Ignited is name of company.<BR>~converts ur car to push button starts<BR>~Pushbutton is illuminated (5 color choices)<BR>~ Easy to install about 30 min. w/o drilling<P>Replaces Cigarette lighter.<P>~Colors                 Stk. No.<BR>Red....................47RL0336T <BR>Blue...................47RL0337A<BR>Yello..................47RL0338R <BR>Green..................47RL0339X <BR>Clear..................47RL0340N<P>1 kit is $69.99<P> Is that good enough?<P><P>------------------<BR>2 85 SE Auto & 4 spd<BR>85 GT Notchback 5 spd<BR>86 GT Fastback Auto<BR>87 SE 5 spd||||reg||1
Z||000020||Elenor84||03-07-2001||07:26 PM||||I went to radio shack today and Picked up about 6 kool little silver switches...one is for the headlights on and off...the next is for the rear defog and 3 are for the three interior lights up above...I'm going to be doing this mod as soon as my car gets out of the shop...I plan on taking out the original plastic holders and switches and replacing them with a silver or crome metal plate...easily cut and put a few holes in it and its good to go...I'll get back to you all wen I'm done...<P>------------------<BR>Lance<BR>Click Image to go to site.<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/_XMCM/lancegspage/Index.html" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Side1.jpg"> </A>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Jay||03-09-2001||06:21 PM||||I've got a push button starter on my car by "Ignited"  The kit is complete with all wiring and instructions.  For security I've got a secret button to press and hold while pushing the starter button.  If this secret button isn't pressed the car won't start.  You could use anything like the door locks/unlock or the rear defroster.  It works great and looks cool too.||||reg||1
